},{"content":[{"level":2,"text":"Discussion","type":"heading","anchor":"discussion"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Each round makes the best improving move, the first move (i, j) in row-major order on","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ties, until no exchange of two tour edges for two lighter ones remains. The first vertex of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"tour","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" stays first. Only ","type":"text"},{"code":"tour.vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is read: the result uses the lightest edge of each","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pair, as every tour does. nil below three vertices.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A move’s gain is formed as (d(a, b) + d(c, e)) − (d(a, c) + d(b, e)), and the scan stops"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"when no move gains anything. With floating-point weights, rounding decides which moves"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"gain."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"style":"note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The graph is complete; ","type":"text"},{"code":"tour.vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" lists every vertex of the graph","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"exactly once; no weight is NaN or infinite; the sum of any two weights fits in ","type":"text"},{"code":"W","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]}],"name":"Precondition","type":"aside"},{"style":"note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(n²) per improving move."}]}],"name":"Complexity","type":"aside"}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/tours\/graphprotocols\/graph\/twoopttour(improving:weight:)"]}],"references":{"doc://Tours/documentation/Tours/GraphProtocols":{"title":"GraphProtocols","identifier":"doc:\/\/Tours\/documentation\/Tours\/GraphProtocols","kind":"symbol","